                <pb facs="0986" n="924" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg924"/>

                <head>No. 528.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Comanos to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Agency and Consulate-General of the United States in
                                Egypt</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Cairo</hi></placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1878-08-08"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >August</hi> 8, 1878</date>. (Received August 30.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 255.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your instructions of the 2d of
                    July last, No. 106, I have the honor to state that there is no other variety of
                    Bamiah cotton save that discovered in the Menonfieh district, which has been
                    already mentioned in Mr. Farman’s dispatch No. 127, of May 4, 1877. Nevertheless
                    I send to your address by this mail two bags of the seed of this plant, which is
                    at present rather scarce.</p>
                <p>The Bamiah plant having bat one stalk requires more frequent watering than the
                    common cotton-plant; and if it once begins to wither the whole plant dies, and
                    the entire fruit is lost. The raising of this species is, therefore, much more
                    risky; and consequently growers, as yet, only dare to experiment with it on
                    small tracts of land. Latterly the results of such experimental culture have not
                    come up to the expectation aroused upon its first discovery.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp; c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>A. D. COMANOS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
